@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "SELECT Operation";
}

<section>

    <!-- PAGE INTRODUCTION -->

    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            SELECT Operation
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            The SELECT statement is used to retrieve data from a
            SQL Server table.
        </p>

        <p>
            SELECT is one of the most important SQL commands.
            It allows us to view records stored in database tables.
            We can retrieve all columns, specific columns, or only
            those records that satisfy a particular condition.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- WHAT IS SELECT -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What is SELECT?
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>SELECT</strong> statement is used to retrieve
            records from one or more tables.
        </p>

        <p>
            It does not modify the existing data. It only reads and
            displays the requested information.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- BASIC SYNTAX -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            SELECT Syntax
        </h2>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT column1, column2
FROM table_name;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            The SELECT clause specifies the columns that we want to
            retrieve, while the FROM clause specifies the table from
            which the data should be retrieved.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- SAMPLE TABLE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Example Table
        </h2>

        <p>
            For the following examples, we will use the Students table.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>StudentId</th>
                        <th>StudentName</th>
                        <th>Age</th>
                        <th>City</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>101</td>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>22</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>102</td>
                        <td>Priya</td>
                        <td>23</td>
                        <td>Mumbai</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>103</td>
                        <td>Amit</td>
                        <td>21</td>
                        <td>Nashik</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>104</td>
                        <td>Neha</td>
                        <td>22</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SELECT ALL COLUMNS -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            SELECT All Columns
        </h2>

        <p>
            To retrieve all columns from a table, we can use the
            asterisk <strong>*</strong>.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- OUTPUT -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Output
        </h2>

        <p>
            The query returns all columns and all records from the
            Students table.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>StudentId</th>
                        <th>StudentName</th>
                        <th>Age</th>
                        <th>City</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>101</td>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>22</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>102</td>
                        <td>Priya</td>
                        <td>23</td>
                        <td>Mumbai</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>103</td>
                        <td>Amit</td>
                        <td>21</td>
                        <td>Nashik</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>104</td>
                        <td>Neha</td>
                        <td>22</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SELECT SPECIFIC COLUMNS -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            SELECT Specific Columns
        </h2>

        <p>
            We can retrieve only the columns that we need instead of
            retrieving all columns.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT StudentName, Age
FROM Students;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SPECIFIC COLUMNS OUTPUT -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Output
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>StudentName</th>
                        <th>Age</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>22</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Priya</td>
                        <td>23</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Amit</td>
                        <td>21</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Neha</td>
                        <td>22</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SELECT WITH WHERE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            SELECT with WHERE
        </h2>

        <p>
            The WHERE clause is used to retrieve only those records
            that satisfy a specified condition.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE City = 'Pune';</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- WHERE OUTPUT -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Output
        </h2>

        <p>
            Only students whose City is Pune are returned.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>StudentId</th>
                        <th>StudentName</th>
                        <th>Age</th>
                        <th>City</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>101</td>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>22</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>104</td>
                        <td>Neha</td>
                        <td>22</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SELECT WITH CONDITION -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            SELECT with Numeric Condition
        </h2>

        <p>
            We can also use comparison operators with numeric columns.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE Age &gt; 21;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ORDER BY -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            SELECT with ORDER BY
        </h2>

        <p>
            ORDER BY is used to sort the retrieved records.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
ORDER BY Age ASC;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            <strong>ASC</strong> sorts the records in ascending order,
            while <strong>DESC</strong> sorts them in descending order.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- DISTINCT -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            SELECT DISTINCT
        </h2>

        <p>
            DISTINCT is used to remove duplicate values from the
            result.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT DISTINCT City
FROM Students;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- PRACTICAL EXAMPLE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Practical Example
        </h2>

        <p>
            Suppose we want to display the names and cities of all
            students from the Students table.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT StudentName, City
FROM Students;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            The result will contain only StudentName and City.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- IMPORTANT POINTS -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-info border-0">

            <h4 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Important Points
            </h4>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    SELECT is used to retrieve data.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    SELECT does not modify existing records.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    The <strong>*</strong> symbol retrieves all columns.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Specific columns can be selected by writing their
                    column names.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    WHERE can be used to filter records.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    ORDER BY can be used to sort records.
                </li>

                <li>
                    DISTINCT can be used to remove duplicate values.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SUMMARY -->

    <section class="mb-4">

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p class="mb-0">
                In this section, we learned how the SELECT statement
                retrieves data from SQL Server tables. We covered
                selecting all columns, selecting specific columns,
                filtering records using WHERE, sorting records using
                ORDER BY and retrieving unique values using DISTINCT.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


